---
intent: problem
section: Context
eyebrow: Context
notes: Set the scene with the numbers, then move on.
---
## Incidents don't wait for office hours
Outages, security events, supplier failures: every team has a plan on paper. Few can run it at 3 am with the people they actually have.

::: cards {.right}
### Every year
**6 organizations out of 10** face a major incident. In those hours, **every minute counts**. {.wide}

### Security
- Ransomware attacks up 12× since 2020.
- One attack every 10 seconds.

### Regulation
- GDPR
- NIS2
- DORA
:::

---
bg: butter
eyebrow: The three pillars
notes: Three words to remember: prepare, react, continue.
---
## Prepare, react, continue
**Prepare** to anticipate and plan, **React** to coordinate and act fast, **Continue** to keep the business running.

::: cards
### Prepare
- **Ready-made plans**, tested.
- **Drills** on a schedule.
- **One place** for procedures.

### React
- **Real-time coordination** of the team.
- **Secure channels**: chat, video, alerts.
- **Faster decisions** with boards and workflows.

### Continue
- **Degraded mode** in one switch.
- **Critical data** available 24/7.
- **Back in business** in under 15 minutes.
:::

---
intent: explain
section: Before
eyebrow: Before the incident
notes: Five steps, in order. This is onboarding.
---
## Before the incident

::: cards {.numbered}
- Create the workspace | Set up the platform and train the core team.
- Enroll the crisis cell | Invite the people who will run the response.
- Switch on what you need | Enable the apps that match your plan.
- Load the playbooks | Procedures, checklists, key documents.
- Rehearse | Run a drill and fix what breaks.
:::

